WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:04.760
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:06.060
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.360
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.600
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.420
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:19.400
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:20.540
We're The Office Ladies.

8
00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:25.350
Oh, hi there.

9
00:00:25.730 --> 00:00:28.290
Angela, can I interest you in a seminar?

10
00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:30.910
It's a podcasting seminar.

11
00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:32.930
Oh, are we going to a seminar?

12
00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:33.470
Yeah.

13
00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:34.690
What do you think?

14
00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:36.090
I put up some flyers.

15
00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:38.850
Is it just going to be us and Sam and Cassie?

16
00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:40.430
Yes, at lunch.

17
00:00:40.630 --> 00:00:41.530
Actually, never mind.

18
00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:42.750
But there will be sandwiches.

19
00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:43.710
There will be sandwiches.

20
00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:44.170
There will.

21
00:00:44.490 --> 00:00:47.090
This is Season 7, Episode 14.

22
00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:48.470
It's called The Seminar.

23
00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:50.410
It was directed by B.J.

24
00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:52.630
Novak and written by Steve Healy.

25
00:00:52.990 --> 00:00:55.590
Steve Healy was a new writer for us in Season 7.

26
00:00:56.110 --> 00:01:00.630
He had previously written for The Late Show with David Letterman and 30 Rock.

27
00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:02.970
He was a co-executive producer on Veep.

28
00:01:03.610 --> 00:01:11.950
I did a little diving on him, and I found out, was reminded, actually, that he wrote this book with Vali Chandra Sokran.

29
00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:13.670
Remember, he played Hot Doctor on Diwali?

30
00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:14.110
Yes.

31
00:01:14.410 --> 00:01:17.270
They wrote a book together called The Ridiculous Race.

32
00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:19.230
I remembered this.

33
00:01:19.910 --> 00:01:21.910
They decided to race around the world.

34
00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:23.210
The two of them?

35
00:01:23.410 --> 00:01:24.530
Just the two of them.

36
00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:26.430
They started in L.A.

37
00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:31.290
They each had to go in different directions, and there was one rule, which was that they couldn't use any planes.

38
00:01:31.690 --> 00:01:33.470
And they had to see who got back to L.A.

39
00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:33.890
firSt.

40
00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:35.830
And they went around the world.

41
00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:36.690
Around the world.

42
00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:40.470
At one point, the way Steve got out of L.A.

43
00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:46.110
was that he arranged for himself to ride on a container ship from Long Beach to Shanghai.

44
00:01:46.790 --> 00:01:47.310
What?

45
00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:47.850
Yeah.

46
00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:50.690
They wrote all about it in their book.

47
00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:52.250
I want to read this book.

48
00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:53.310
It's insane, right?

49
00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:55.230
I mean, a container ship.

50
00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:57.890
Anything can happen out there.

51
00:01:57.890 --> 00:01:58.190
No.

52
00:01:58.290 --> 00:01:59.710
You're out in the wide open ocean.

53
00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:02.950
I mean, how do you do that?

54
00:02:03.230 --> 00:02:06.370
How do you convince someone to let you ride on a container ship?

55
00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:07.910
I don't know.

56
00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:10.090
I guess I'll have to read the book.

57
00:02:10.890 --> 00:02:16.190
Steve also wrote a book called The Wonder Trail, where he traveled through Central and South America.

58
00:02:16.810 --> 00:02:23.770
And he also wrote a novel called How I Became a Famous Novelist, which won the 2010 Thurber Prize for American Humor.

59
00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:25.650
That's so cool.

60
00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:28.590
Yeah, so he's been writing a lot of books since we saw him.

61
00:02:29.710 --> 00:02:31.670
But he also wrote this episode.

62
00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:32.750
Would you like a summary?

63
00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:33.430
Yes.

64
00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:41.150
As a ploy to get new clients, Andy hosts a business seminar in the conference room and enlists the help of his fellow employees.

65
00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:45.830
Jim mysteriously skips out after recognizing one of the attendees.

66
00:02:46.570 --> 00:02:50.770
Michael and Holly start to reconnect by pretending to be a Greek couple.

67
00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:56.030
Oscar and Pam help Aaron play Gabe in an online game of Scrabble.

68
00:02:56.270 --> 00:02:57.870
Well, Jenna, fantastic summary.

69
00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:00.650
I know you have some hot fast facts for us.

70
00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:03.350
And after that, I have a little digital clutter.

71
00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:04.910
Well, buckle in, everyone.

72
00:03:05.070 --> 00:03:05.830
Let's get started.

73
00:03:06.710 --> 00:03:07.990
Fast fact number one.

74
00:03:08.530 --> 00:03:14.870
This episode aired on January 27th, 2011, and it won its time slot for the night.

75
00:03:15.070 --> 00:03:15.510
Again.

76
00:03:16.150 --> 00:03:16.370
Woo.

77
00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:17.350
Way to go, office.

78
00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:18.170
Bloop, bloop, bloop, bloop.

79
00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:27.910
Before this episode aired, they had just announced to the press that Will Ferrell would be joining us for a four-episode arc at the end of this season.

80
00:03:28.610 --> 00:03:30.010
And there was a lot of buzz.

81
00:03:30.170 --> 00:03:30.990
Do you remember the buzz?

82
00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:32.370
We were so excited.

83
00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:33.730
Oh, my gosh.

84
00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:35.450
I mean, we were, like, sad excited.

85
00:03:35.690 --> 00:03:36.890
We were like, oh, no, Steve.

86
00:03:37.030 --> 00:03:37.810
Oh, Will Ferrell.

87
00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:38.190
Yes.

88
00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:38.390
Oh.

89
00:03:39.050 --> 00:03:41.390
So they released this news on purpose.

90
00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:42.910
They wanted the buzz.

91
00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:45.270
It was a bit of a red herring.

92
00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:46.070
Oh.

93
00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:47.390
Intrigue.

94
00:03:47.470 --> 00:03:50.530
They were trying to distract from something else.

95
00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:51.930
And what was the other thing?

96
00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:53.230
I know you're going to tell us.

97
00:03:53.230 --> 00:04:04.670
They were trying to distract everyone from the big cameo guest star that kicks off this episode, Ricky Gervais, as David Brent, has come to Scranton.

98
00:04:04.970 --> 00:04:07.630
David Brent and Michael Scott meet.

99
00:04:07.630 --> 00:04:11.970
I mean, for any office fan, this is a huge moment.

100
00:04:12.290 --> 00:04:12.890
Huge.

101
00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:15.070
And that is fast fact number two.

102
00:04:15.210 --> 00:04:20.370
We're going to break down this cold open and tell you all about Ricky coming to the office.

103
00:04:20.570 --> 00:04:21.290
Kick it off.

104
00:04:21.290 --> 00:04:24.090
Well, it all starts at some elevator bays.

105
00:04:24.590 --> 00:04:24.850
Yeah.

106
00:04:25.070 --> 00:04:31.890
Michael runs into a very nice English fella who's getting off the elevator who says his name is David Brent.

107
00:04:32.090 --> 00:04:35.270
Michael immediately does a British accent, or his attempt to.

108
00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:39.930
And then David Brent shares that he also does accents and characters.

109
00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:44.810
And then they go back and forth with their various characters that they do.

110
00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:46.570
They are delighting one another.

111
00:04:46.750 --> 00:04:47.610
Oh, my gosh.

112
00:04:47.710 --> 00:04:48.630
Kindred spirits.

113
00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:52.970
David then says, comedy is a place where the mind goes to tickle itself.

114
00:04:53.410 --> 00:04:54.590
That's what she said.

115
00:04:55.130 --> 00:04:56.590
Michael can't believe it.

116
00:04:56.750 --> 00:04:57.890
This is his soulmate.

117
00:04:58.110 --> 00:05:00.290
You thought Holly was his soulmate.

118
00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:01.730
Here it is.

119
00:05:02.250 --> 00:05:02.650
Yeah.

120
00:05:03.170 --> 00:05:04.410
Michael hugs him.

121
00:05:04.590 --> 00:05:04.910
Goodbye.

122
00:05:05.330 --> 00:05:05.690
Yes.

123
00:05:05.850 --> 00:05:07.850
And then David says, are you guys hiring?

124
00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:09.110
He said, no.

125
00:05:09.530 --> 00:05:09.850
Mm-hmm.

126
00:05:10.070 --> 00:05:12.110
And then he said, well, think of me if something comes up.

127
00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:13.330
And he leaves.

128
00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:14.750
And Michael's like, what a nice guy.

129
00:05:15.330 --> 00:05:17.550
I'm sure you can imagine we got a lot of mail about this.

130
00:05:17.710 --> 00:05:18.210
Oh, yeah.

131
00:05:18.650 --> 00:05:19.530
I'll kick it off.

132
00:05:19.930 --> 00:05:21.410
First up is Karen C.

133
00:05:21.590 --> 00:05:27.630
from Delaware, as well as many others, who would like to know, where was the cold open supposed to be?

134
00:05:27.690 --> 00:05:31.250
Because it doesn't look like the industrial park where Dunder Mifflin is located.

135
00:05:31.510 --> 00:05:32.990
Karen, I feel seen.

136
00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:35.690
I wrote in my notes, where the heck is this elevator?

137
00:05:35.690 --> 00:05:40.850
Well, while we were shooting the episode The Sting, you remember that one where they meet Danny Cordray?

138
00:05:40.850 --> 00:05:41.150
Oh, yeah.

139
00:05:41.570 --> 00:05:46.790
Well, Ricky Gervais happened to be in town for, I don't know, a celebrity appearance or something.

140
00:05:46.850 --> 00:05:50.630
And he just volunteered to come in and do a scene for us.

141
00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:52.590
He said, I want to do a scene with Steve.

142
00:05:53.250 --> 00:05:53.630
Awesome.

143
00:05:53.950 --> 00:05:54.110
Yeah.

144
00:05:54.530 --> 00:05:57.670
So they were like, well, we're at this location.

145
00:05:57.890 --> 00:05:59.190
Why don't you come on down?

146
00:05:59.370 --> 00:06:02.490
So we shot it there at that same place, at those same elevators.

147
00:06:02.490 --> 00:06:05.550
You know, the one where Michael wonders if the buttons even work?

148
00:06:05.690 --> 00:06:05.990
Mm-hmm.

149
00:06:06.550 --> 00:06:09.450
Randy said he had to create a stealth call sheet.

150
00:06:09.810 --> 00:06:11.630
He did not put Ricky's name down.

151
00:06:11.750 --> 00:06:13.530
They did not want this cameo to get out.

152
00:06:13.690 --> 00:06:15.590
They lied to NBC about it.

153
00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:17.530
They have a habit of doing that.

154
00:06:17.690 --> 00:06:18.090
Yes.

155
00:06:18.230 --> 00:06:19.630
They didn't want any promos.

156
00:06:19.750 --> 00:06:22.290
That's why they did the whole Will Ferrell press release.

157
00:06:22.450 --> 00:06:22.930
All of it.

158
00:06:23.270 --> 00:06:24.210
But guess what?

159
00:06:24.370 --> 00:06:24.630
What?

160
00:06:25.130 --> 00:06:28.290
The week before the episode aired, it got out.

161
00:06:28.650 --> 00:06:31.670
Hollywood Reporter ran a story that Ricky was going to be on.

162
00:06:31.670 --> 00:06:35.510
And Paul Lieberstein was even quoted for the episode, but he was very careful.

163
00:06:35.510 --> 00:06:39.910
He wouldn't say how or why Ricky was going to be on the show.

164
00:06:39.930 --> 00:06:41.150
So it did leak a little bit.

165
00:06:41.630 --> 00:06:42.690
But we tried.

166
00:06:42.970 --> 00:06:44.110
We tried not to leak.

167
00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:46.390
We made it all the way to the week before.

168
00:06:46.870 --> 00:06:49.450
Next up, we have a fan question from Amanda C.

169
00:06:49.670 --> 00:06:51.450
in Indiana, who says,

170
00:06:51.550 --> 00:06:54.590
I just have to know how much the cold open was scripted.

171
00:06:54.710 --> 00:06:59.470
It almost seems like Ricky is genuinely breaking and reacting to Steve's version of Michael.

172
00:06:59.470 --> 00:07:01.970
His laugh just seems so genuine.

173
00:07:02.290 --> 00:07:05.250
I want to know everything about their interaction while filming.

174
00:07:05.750 --> 00:07:11.450
Well, Amanda, there was a script for this cold open between the two of them.

175
00:07:11.790 --> 00:07:15.530
Our writers got together and wrote 11 versions of this scene.

176
00:07:16.030 --> 00:07:19.110
In the scripted version, they were on the elevator together.

177
00:07:19.450 --> 00:07:23.010
Like, one of them would be on and the other would enter and this would start the conversation.

178
00:07:23.510 --> 00:07:27.970
But when they got there on the day, they kind of realized, you know what?

179
00:07:28.070 --> 00:07:29.890
These two guys are really funny.

180
00:07:30.210 --> 00:07:32.630
We're just going to let them talk outside this elevator bay.

181
00:07:32.770 --> 00:07:33.870
And that's what they did.

182
00:07:34.350 --> 00:07:40.030
I reached out to our editor, Dave Rogers, and I was like, Dave, were there any alts for this scene?

183
00:07:40.150 --> 00:07:41.070
I mean, come on.

184
00:07:41.250 --> 00:07:44.690
And he said, you know, Ange, we put the best stuff in the bloopers.

185
00:07:44.970 --> 00:07:48.170
It was mostly Steve cracking Ricky up that day.

186
00:07:48.170 --> 00:07:49.990
That's what I remember hearing.

187
00:07:50.250 --> 00:07:53.190
Dave went on to say that he wasn't there the day they filmed this,

188
00:07:53.330 --> 00:07:58.430
but he really remembers editing the bloopers so clearly because Ricky's laugh is so loud.

189
00:07:58.690 --> 00:07:59.130
Uh-huh.

190
00:07:59.570 --> 00:08:03.590
He said at one point, Ricky even said, I'm not even a real actor.

191
00:08:05.450 --> 00:08:07.390
I just love how much fun they were having.

192
00:08:07.490 --> 00:08:08.650
I thought we should hear the bloopers.

193
00:08:08.950 --> 00:08:10.450
I'm working on an English character.

194
00:08:10.650 --> 00:08:11.150
Would you mind?

195
00:08:11.610 --> 00:08:13.370
It's called Reginald Poofta.

196
00:08:20.920 --> 00:08:21.700
Sorry, mate.

197
00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:25.520
I forgot me snoted cream.

198
00:08:27.490 --> 00:08:29.090
I'm so sorry.

199
00:08:29.450 --> 00:08:29.870
Right.

200
00:08:30.190 --> 00:08:30.690
Okay.

201
00:08:31.050 --> 00:08:32.370
This is no good.

202
00:08:32.670 --> 00:08:33.850
I'm not even a real actor.

203
00:08:33.970 --> 00:08:34.490
Let me out.

204
00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:40.360
All right.

205
00:08:40.420 --> 00:08:43.360
We have one more fan question for this cold open.

206
00:08:43.580 --> 00:08:44.880
It's from Mel W.

207
00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:46.620
in Calgary, Alberta, Canada.

208
00:08:46.900 --> 00:08:47.860
Mel, what you got?

209
00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:51.280
When Michael Scott meets David Brent waiting for an elevator,

210
00:08:51.520 --> 00:08:55.000
one thing that always seemed off, considering the fact that David Brent himself

211
00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:57.880
had once been in a documentary in the U.K.,

212
00:08:57.880 --> 00:09:01.880
is why didn't he acknowledge or comment on the fact that there was a camera crew

213
00:09:01.880 --> 00:09:03.280
standing there filming Michael?

214
00:09:03.760 --> 00:09:07.660
It seems like a major opportunity for David to talk about his own documentary

215
00:09:07.660 --> 00:09:10.740
or to say something idiotic to the camera like he used to.

216
00:09:11.500 --> 00:09:13.680
Mel, this is so smart.

217
00:09:13.940 --> 00:09:14.180
Yeah.

218
00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:16.820
I didn't even think of it when I was watching it.

219
00:09:16.940 --> 00:09:21.180
But of course, David Brent would be very savvy to the camera crew.

220
00:09:21.180 --> 00:09:24.560
Unless we don't know when in time this happened.

221
00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:26.120
What do you mean?

222
00:09:26.300 --> 00:09:32.940
What if this scene happened in the canon of the show before David Brent got a camera crew?

223
00:09:33.060 --> 00:09:35.340
Maybe this inspired David Brent to be like,

224
00:09:35.480 --> 00:09:38.220
will you come film me at my job in Slough?

225
00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:39.480
Oh my gosh.

226
00:09:39.620 --> 00:09:45.040
My mind is bending right now because you're suggesting that maybe Dunder Mifflin happened

227
00:09:46.540 --> 00:09:48.680
before Wernam Hogg happened.

228
00:09:48.680 --> 00:09:48.980
Yeah.

229
00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:52.220
And that Dunder Mifflin and Michael Scott and his camera crew

230
00:09:52.220 --> 00:09:55.620
are what inspired David Brent to want to do the same thing.

231
00:09:56.180 --> 00:09:56.460
Yeah.

232
00:09:56.760 --> 00:09:57.760
I feel that.

233
00:09:57.920 --> 00:09:59.440
I feel him going back and being like,

234
00:09:59.500 --> 00:10:00.680
I met a guy in America.

235
00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:02.360
They follow him around.

236
00:10:02.460 --> 00:10:03.100
With a camera.

237
00:10:03.700 --> 00:10:04.660
I mean, come on.

238
00:10:04.760 --> 00:10:06.660
Hey, BBC One, I have an idea for you.

239
00:10:08.020 --> 00:10:08.460
Wow.

240
00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:09.480
Good one, Ange.

241
00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:10.100
You're welcome.

242
00:10:10.400 --> 00:10:11.880
You guys, there is no spoon.

243
00:10:12.780 --> 00:10:13.220
Oh.

244
00:10:13.460 --> 00:10:14.340
There is no spoon.

245
00:10:15.140 --> 00:10:16.260
We love you, Kiana.

246
00:10:16.360 --> 00:10:16.820
We love you.

247
00:10:17.380 --> 00:10:19.040
All right, fast fact number three.

248
00:10:19.140 --> 00:10:19.600
Are you ready?

249
00:10:19.880 --> 00:10:20.340
I'm ready.

250
00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:20.780
It's a deep dive.

251
00:10:20.980 --> 00:10:22.700
And it's a deep dive on Scrabble.

252
00:10:23.060 --> 00:10:23.720
The game?

253
00:10:23.880 --> 00:10:25.240
The game, because that's...

254
00:10:25.240 --> 00:10:27.060
That's what Gabe and Aaron are playing.

255
00:10:27.160 --> 00:10:27.480
Yeah.

256
00:10:28.700 --> 00:10:29.880
I love Scrabble.

257
00:10:29.920 --> 00:10:32.240
We just played it last weekend as our family game night.

258
00:10:32.440 --> 00:10:35.800
Well, every once in a while, I'll Google something that appears in an episode.

259
00:10:35.800 --> 00:10:37.640
And if it's interesting, I want to share.

260
00:10:37.880 --> 00:10:43.740
So I Googled Time Magazine, Britannia.com, Wikipedia, and the New York Times.

261
00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:45.040
And here's what they told me.

262
00:10:45.820 --> 00:10:52.720
Scrabble was originally invented by an American architect named Alfred Mosher Butts in 1938.

263
00:10:53.460 --> 00:10:55.820
And he called it crisscross words.

264
00:10:56.700 --> 00:10:57.720
Not Scrabble.

265
00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:00.480
What else did Mr. Mosher Butts say?

266
00:11:01.160 --> 00:11:06.240
After he manufactured a few sets on his own, he was not able to sell it anywhere.

267
00:11:06.500 --> 00:11:07.520
No one wanted it.

268
00:11:07.820 --> 00:11:09.500
No one wanted crisscross words.

269
00:11:09.760 --> 00:11:11.660
Oh, Albert, you were so onto something.

270
00:11:11.940 --> 00:11:12.880
People didn't know.

271
00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:20.240
In 1948, a man named James Brunot, or Brunote, who was a resident of Newtown, Connecticut,

272
00:11:21.460 --> 00:11:23.740
he bought a crisscross words.

273
00:11:24.060 --> 00:11:25.980
He was like, I'm going to buy this game.

274
00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:27.180
And he loved it.

275
00:11:27.580 --> 00:11:33.460
In fact, he asked if he could buy the rights to manufacture the game in exchange for giving

276
00:11:33.460 --> 00:11:35.880
Butts a royalty on every unit sold.

277
00:11:36.440 --> 00:11:41.040
So he left most of the game unchanged, but he did do a couple of things.

278
00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:43.500
He rearranged the premium squares on the board.

279
00:11:44.040 --> 00:11:45.800
He simplified the rules a little bit.

280
00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:49.040
And he's the one who renamed the game Scrabble.

281
00:11:49.700 --> 00:11:51.280
Sometimes that's all in a name.

282
00:11:51.560 --> 00:11:53.820
Did you know that Scrabble is a real word?

283
00:11:54.120 --> 00:11:54.620
No.

284
00:11:55.100 --> 00:11:57.400
It means to scratch frenetically.

285
00:11:58.000 --> 00:11:58.600
No.

286
00:11:58.980 --> 00:12:05.100
Which is odd that you named this game about having to scratch a lot.

287
00:12:05.740 --> 00:12:06.180
Scrabble.

288
00:12:06.660 --> 00:12:07.100
Scrabble.

289
00:12:07.500 --> 00:12:07.940
Yeah.

290
00:12:08.020 --> 00:12:09.800
I was up all last night scrabbling.

291
00:12:10.020 --> 00:12:11.660
It means you were scratching yourself.

292
00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:13.440
So interesting.

293
00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:14.580
I need to scrabble.

294
00:12:14.780 --> 00:12:15.980
I have poison ivy.

295
00:12:16.100 --> 00:12:17.280
I need to scrabble.

296
00:12:17.740 --> 00:12:18.780
I don't get it.

297
00:12:18.820 --> 00:12:19.360
I don't get it.

298
00:12:19.420 --> 00:12:21.700
I mean, I always thought scrabble meant when things were jumbled.

299
00:12:22.060 --> 00:12:22.340
Right?

300
00:12:22.580 --> 00:12:22.800
Yeah.

301
00:12:22.960 --> 00:12:23.440
I don't know.

302
00:12:24.280 --> 00:12:24.920
All right.

303
00:12:25.180 --> 00:12:32.000
So, Brunat and his family converted a former schoolhouse into their production facility.

304
00:12:32.040 --> 00:12:34.660
And they started making a whole bunch of these games.

305
00:12:34.820 --> 00:12:35.360
Guess what?

306
00:12:35.540 --> 00:12:35.800
What?

307
00:12:35.960 --> 00:12:36.960
No one wanted them.

308
00:12:37.120 --> 00:12:37.680
Oh, no.

309
00:12:37.860 --> 00:12:38.020
No.

310
00:12:38.020 --> 00:12:38.740
Did he go bankrupt?

311
00:12:39.140 --> 00:12:39.540
No.

312
00:12:40.200 --> 00:12:46.880
Because in 1952, Jack Strauss, who was the president of Macy's, played the game on vacation.

313
00:12:47.480 --> 00:12:50.780
They had given some out to, like, hotels and stuff, I guess.

314
00:12:50.880 --> 00:12:51.280
Game changer.

315
00:12:51.280 --> 00:12:51.800
Yeah.

316
00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:53.360
He loved it.

317
00:12:53.420 --> 00:12:55.680
He couldn't believe that no one was selling this game.

318
00:12:55.880 --> 00:12:57.740
He said, I'm going to sell it at Macy's.

319
00:12:57.940 --> 00:12:59.420
Everyone has to have one.

320
00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:00.800
Guess what?

321
00:13:01.120 --> 00:13:03.780
Within a year, it had gone, I guess.

322
00:13:03.780 --> 00:13:04.700
Number one game.

323
00:13:04.880 --> 00:13:05.300
Viral.

324
00:13:05.480 --> 00:13:05.660
Yeah.

325
00:13:05.760 --> 00:13:05.900
Yeah.

326
00:13:06.180 --> 00:13:08.000
Back in ye olde version of viral.

327
00:13:08.700 --> 00:13:13.480
Since then, it has been inducted into the National Toy Hall of Fame.

328
00:13:13.820 --> 00:13:16.160
And the game is sold in 121 countries.

329
00:13:16.380 --> 00:13:18.740
It is available in more than 30 languages.

330
00:13:18.740 --> 00:13:25.520
The original inventor, Mr. Butts, said that he earned about three cents per game in royalties.

331
00:13:25.820 --> 00:13:26.840
Here's what he said.

332
00:13:27.160 --> 00:13:34.520
One-third went to taxes, one-third went to charity, and the last third enabled him to have an enjoyable life.

333
00:13:34.920 --> 00:13:35.440
Hey.

334
00:13:35.440 --> 00:13:39.000
He also said that his wife was the best player of the game.

335
00:13:39.160 --> 00:13:43.640
She once scored 234 points for the word quixotic.

336
00:13:44.420 --> 00:13:44.980
Wow.

337
00:13:45.380 --> 00:13:47.720
Q-U-I-X-O-T-I-C.

338
00:13:48.320 --> 00:13:49.280
Which means?

339
00:13:49.920 --> 00:13:50.740
Don't know.

340
00:13:51.960 --> 00:13:53.460
I want to know what it means.

341
00:13:53.660 --> 00:13:54.460
I'll look it up.

342
00:13:54.720 --> 00:13:55.860
Googling music, please.

343
00:13:59.760 --> 00:14:00.640
Okay, ready?

344
00:14:03.180 --> 00:14:04.320
All right.

345
00:14:06.420 --> 00:14:11.680
Quixotic means exceedingly idealistic, unrealistic, and impractical.

346
00:14:11.940 --> 00:14:13.860
Can you use it in a sentence, please?

347
00:14:14.420 --> 00:14:20.300
One might say creating a new board game is a quixotic project.

348
00:14:20.660 --> 00:14:22.660
Way to bring it full circle, Fisher.

349
00:14:22.980 --> 00:14:23.420
Mm-hmm.

350
00:14:25.000 --> 00:14:26.740
That's all I got, Ange.

351
00:14:26.900 --> 00:14:32.320
Well, I have a Kinsey tidbit, and I have a digital clutter moment for you.

352
00:14:32.620 --> 00:14:32.840
Wow.

353
00:14:33.260 --> 00:14:38.460
Kinsey tidbit is, the alt name for this episode was called The Closer.

354
00:14:39.020 --> 00:14:39.600
Hmm.

355
00:14:39.780 --> 00:14:40.940
And not The Seminar.

356
00:14:41.320 --> 00:14:41.600
Okay.

357
00:14:41.800 --> 00:14:45.720
So when you go back to shooting drafts, you'll see it being called The Closer.

358
00:14:46.020 --> 00:14:46.860
I don't know.

359
00:14:46.920 --> 00:14:47.940
I always find that interesting.

360
00:14:48.260 --> 00:14:49.420
I do as well.

361
00:14:50.040 --> 00:14:55.800
And before we go to break, I just wanted to tell you guys that as I was reading the

362
00:14:55.800 --> 00:15:00.780
shooting draft for this episode, I noticed the shooting draft was dated October 21st,

363
00:15:00.940 --> 00:15:01.740
2010.

364
00:15:02.320 --> 00:15:05.580
We shot this before we went on our holiday break.

365
00:15:07.160 --> 00:15:08.240
What happened?

366
00:15:08.480 --> 00:15:09.080
What happened?

367
00:15:09.100 --> 00:15:12.480
So I was like, what were we doing that week in October?

368
00:15:12.480 --> 00:15:13.740
I was curious.

369
00:15:14.280 --> 00:15:14.840
Okay.

370
00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:16.800
Here's what my digital clutter revealed.

371
00:15:16.800 --> 00:15:20.360
You and I were in the press that week.

372
00:15:20.520 --> 00:15:21.000
What?

373
00:15:21.520 --> 00:15:22.720
Why, Angela?

374
00:15:23.560 --> 00:15:28.020
I was nominated for an award, and you were my plus one.

375
00:15:28.400 --> 00:15:30.800
What was the award, and where did we go?

376
00:15:31.040 --> 00:15:33.120
Here is what the press statement said.

377
00:15:33.120 --> 00:15:40.780
The sixth annual La Femme Film Festival Awards will be honoring Angela Bassett with the Pioneer

378
00:15:40.780 --> 00:15:46.960
Award, Virginia Madsen with the Visionary Award, Angela Kinsey with the New Establishment

379
00:15:46.960 --> 00:15:52.720
Award, Loretta Devine with the Thespian Award, and Maggie Wheeler with the Comedic Actress

380
00:15:52.720 --> 00:15:57.860
and Television Award at the festival's award ceremony, October 17th, 2010.

381
00:15:58.360 --> 00:15:58.940
Hmm.

382
00:15:58.940 --> 00:16:01.660
I found a video from the red carpet.

383
00:16:01.940 --> 00:16:02.360
No.

384
00:16:02.800 --> 00:16:03.320
Yes.

385
00:16:03.720 --> 00:16:08.120
But I'm really bummed because I couldn't find any pictures of us, and I know there are some

386
00:16:08.120 --> 00:16:08.480
out there.

387
00:16:08.580 --> 00:16:09.420
I looked everywhere.

388
00:16:09.720 --> 00:16:14.660
I found an email exchange between you and I, and we were debating about my dress, and

389
00:16:14.660 --> 00:16:18.840
we decided on this sort of deep purple strapless number with an updo.

390
00:16:19.120 --> 00:16:19.600
Okay.

391
00:16:20.140 --> 00:16:26.540
I love how we would always consult one another on these choices, as if either of us are an

392
00:16:26.540 --> 00:16:28.320
expert in fashion or style.

393
00:16:28.320 --> 00:16:29.300
I am telling you.

394
00:16:29.480 --> 00:16:32.540
Why did we not get other opinions?

395
00:16:32.740 --> 00:16:34.400
I mean, we are the worst people to ask.

396
00:16:34.460 --> 00:16:34.960
I know.

397
00:16:35.060 --> 00:16:40.160
You and I are, like, right now in sweatpants and, like, baggy, like, hoodies.

398
00:16:40.300 --> 00:16:41.000
Like, ridiculous.

399
00:16:41.020 --> 00:16:44.940
We are no fashion plates, and, you know, it's a real struggle for us.

400
00:16:45.020 --> 00:16:48.120
But yet, you helped me craft many a red carpet look.

401
00:16:48.200 --> 00:16:48.560
Same.

402
00:16:49.100 --> 00:16:49.660
All right.

403
00:16:49.780 --> 00:16:50.680
Here it is.

404
00:16:51.660 --> 00:16:52.660
And who are we talking to?

405
00:16:52.800 --> 00:16:53.020
Hi.

406
00:16:53.100 --> 00:16:55.120
I'm Angela Kinsey from NBC's The Office.

407
00:16:55.880 --> 00:16:57.780
And you've got to be excited to be here tonight.

408
00:16:58.020 --> 00:16:58.780
It didn't rain.

409
00:16:59.040 --> 00:16:59.480
I know.

410
00:16:59.700 --> 00:17:01.380
And you've got quite a few people turning out.

411
00:17:01.840 --> 00:17:03.520
Tell me about the La Femme Film Festival.

412
00:17:03.640 --> 00:17:04.260
Is this your first time?

413
00:17:04.400 --> 00:17:08.280
This is my first time, and I'm being honored with the new Establishment Award.

414
00:17:08.580 --> 00:17:10.020
I'm really excited about it.

415
00:17:10.020 --> 00:17:14.580
I love that this festival brings attention to women in the industry, whether they're filmmakers

416
00:17:14.580 --> 00:17:17.100
or they're in front of the camera or behind the camera.

417
00:17:17.400 --> 00:17:20.580
And on our show, we have a lot of women in pivotal positions.

418
00:17:20.580 --> 00:17:22.300
We have a camera operator.

419
00:17:22.480 --> 00:17:23.920
We have writers, producers, directors.

420
00:17:24.500 --> 00:17:26.400
Our first and second AD are women.

421
00:17:26.420 --> 00:17:28.840
So I was really passionate about being part of this.

422
00:17:30.400 --> 00:17:30.900
Angela.

423
00:17:31.440 --> 00:17:32.280
I know.

424
00:17:32.960 --> 00:17:33.920
How about that?

425
00:17:33.920 --> 00:17:37.100
The tiny feminist in me is so proud of you for that soundbite.

426
00:17:38.060 --> 00:17:40.060
Way to raise up the ladies on our show.

427
00:17:40.260 --> 00:17:40.520
Yeah.

428
00:17:40.720 --> 00:17:42.460
And my award is really beautiful.

429
00:17:42.460 --> 00:17:43.320
I still have it.

430
00:17:43.380 --> 00:17:44.280
It's like on my shelf.

431
00:17:44.420 --> 00:17:46.280
It's like this big crystal-looking award.

432
00:17:46.540 --> 00:17:48.640
So that is what we were doing.

433
00:17:49.420 --> 00:17:49.900
Wow.

434
00:17:50.940 --> 00:17:52.360
With that, let's go to break.

435
00:17:52.360 --> 00:17:58.240
Because when we get back, you guys, someone here at Office Ladies got their very own microphone.

436
00:17:58.920 --> 00:17:59.980
I can't wait.

437
00:17:59.980 --> 00:18:09.430
We are back.

438
00:18:09.550 --> 00:18:11.350
And Sam pointed out something.

439
00:18:11.630 --> 00:18:13.110
Sam, I can't believe we didn't think of this.

440
00:18:13.610 --> 00:18:16.890
Sam, you said it's been 146 episodes.

441
00:18:17.070 --> 00:18:18.630
I was editing the last episode.

442
00:18:18.770 --> 00:18:22.070
And every time you talk to me and Cassie, it's me clearly on the mic.

443
00:18:22.210 --> 00:18:24.170
And then Cassie very faintly yelling.

444
00:18:24.530 --> 00:18:25.530
So Cassie, you have a mic now.

445
00:18:25.890 --> 00:18:26.870
Yay, Cassie!

446
00:18:27.010 --> 00:18:27.990
Say hi to everybody!

447
00:18:28.250 --> 00:18:29.070
Hello, everyone.

448
00:18:29.570 --> 00:18:29.670
Yay!

449
00:18:30.030 --> 00:18:31.070
Listen to that!

450
00:18:31.290 --> 00:18:35.930
So I took 146 episodes to occur to me that this studio is full of microphones.

451
00:18:35.930 --> 00:18:38.310
We could totally put one where you sit.

452
00:18:38.590 --> 00:18:39.190
Thank you.

453
00:18:39.370 --> 00:18:39.970
Thank you, Sam.

454
00:18:40.250 --> 00:18:41.910
Well, how's this episode start, Angela?

455
00:18:42.250 --> 00:18:48.270
It's going to start with Andy coming in and Aaron very excitedly telling him that his first student has arrived.

456
00:18:48.790 --> 00:18:49.230
Yes.

457
00:18:49.570 --> 00:18:56.750
Andy has a talking head where he explains that he attended a seminar last year called the 10 Secrets of Real Estate,

458
00:18:56.970 --> 00:19:00.330
which ended up being a scam to get him to buy a timeshare in Idaho.

459
00:19:00.330 --> 00:19:07.190
But what he realized is that he could throw his own scam seminar to get clients.

460
00:19:07.650 --> 00:19:07.890
Yeah.

461
00:19:08.370 --> 00:19:11.690
I was curious what the 10 Secrets of Real Estate were.

462
00:19:11.990 --> 00:19:14.170
Oh, so I had to find out.

463
00:19:14.790 --> 00:19:17.330
According to the internet, this is what Andy would have heard.

464
00:19:17.810 --> 00:19:19.550
Number one, know your market.

465
00:19:20.230 --> 00:19:21.950
Number two, be responsive.

466
00:19:22.430 --> 00:19:24.530
Number three, listen to your clients.

467
00:19:25.170 --> 00:19:27.410
Number four, manage their expectations.

468
00:19:27.410 --> 00:19:30.310
Number five, list like a pro.

469
00:19:31.530 --> 00:19:33.490
Number six, build a contact liSt.

470
00:19:33.870 --> 00:19:35.050
Number seven, never-

471
00:19:35.050 --> 00:19:36.910
Number six is build a contact list?

472
00:19:36.910 --> 00:19:37.910
Yes, it's a little late.

473
00:19:38.150 --> 00:19:44.110
I mean, I don't want to tell the real estate people their business, but I feel like you might want to lead with that.

474
00:19:44.130 --> 00:19:44.930
You might want to bump that up.

475
00:19:45.170 --> 00:19:48.530
Number seven, never stop marketing.

476
00:19:49.170 --> 00:19:49.630
Okay.

477
00:19:49.970 --> 00:19:52.350
Number eight, negotiate everything.

478
00:19:53.470 --> 00:19:55.270
Number nine, carve out a niche.

479
00:19:55.910 --> 00:19:58.050
Number 10, ask for reviews.

480
00:19:59.330 --> 00:20:03.090
These are some of the secrets to being a top-notch real estate agent.

481
00:20:03.510 --> 00:20:10.330
I have to bring up my favorite Instagram account that lists houses called Zillow Gone Wild on Instagram.

482
00:20:10.990 --> 00:20:11.930
Come on, guys.

483
00:20:11.930 --> 00:20:13.070
It's so good.

484
00:20:13.270 --> 00:20:14.170
Have you seen this, Jenna?

485
00:20:14.310 --> 00:20:15.050
Zillow Gone Wild?

486
00:20:15.210 --> 00:20:15.530
No.

487
00:20:15.530 --> 00:20:18.970
Okay, it's an Instagram account of houses that are for sale.

488
00:20:19.230 --> 00:20:19.610
Okay.

489
00:20:19.910 --> 00:20:26.890
And a lot of them look really normal from the outside, but then when you do the carousel of pictures, there's always something wacky.

490
00:20:27.010 --> 00:20:29.790
There's like a basement that looks like a dungeon.

491
00:20:29.950 --> 00:20:33.050
Or there was one, the whole house looked like Tron the movie.

492
00:20:33.430 --> 00:20:34.490
Oh, my gosh.

493
00:20:34.710 --> 00:20:36.610
It's like, you know, the website Zillow.

494
00:20:36.850 --> 00:20:38.170
But if Zillow went wild.

495
00:20:38.170 --> 00:20:38.750
It's gone wild.

496
00:20:39.150 --> 00:20:40.170
Zillow Gone Wild.

497
00:20:40.190 --> 00:20:41.010
You have to check it out.

498
00:20:41.670 --> 00:20:45.190
More students are going to arrive, including Mykonos.

499
00:20:45.930 --> 00:20:46.450
Yes.

500
00:20:47.550 --> 00:20:49.470
Mykonos, he is a Greek man.

501
00:20:50.050 --> 00:20:50.830
Oh, wait.

502
00:20:50.830 --> 00:20:53.950
It's Michael Scott, pretending to be a Greek man.

503
00:20:54.030 --> 00:20:55.030
But Erin's not sure.

504
00:20:55.270 --> 00:20:58.010
Michael gives a talking head where he explains he's the plant.

505
00:20:58.190 --> 00:20:59.670
Every great seminar has one.

506
00:20:59.810 --> 00:21:02.010
And I had a little observation here, Angela.

507
00:21:02.270 --> 00:21:08.530
I think that Mykonos bears a strong resemblance to Michael Scott season one with the hair slicked back.

508
00:21:08.530 --> 00:21:09.490
Oh, and the v-neck.

509
00:21:09.730 --> 00:21:10.010
Yeah.

510
00:21:10.850 --> 00:21:12.390
I was like, oh, my gosh.

511
00:21:12.390 --> 00:21:13.810
It's season one all over again.

512
00:21:14.390 --> 00:21:18.690
I don't know about you, but I felt like I heard a little bit of grue in Mykonos.

513
00:21:18.870 --> 00:21:20.010
Tiny bit of grue.

514
00:21:20.070 --> 00:21:21.270
Tiny bit of grue from Minions.

515
00:21:21.370 --> 00:21:21.870
I'm with you.

516
00:21:22.130 --> 00:21:22.450
Mm-hmm.

517
00:21:23.230 --> 00:21:26.770
Andy has recruited a bunch of people to help with his seminar.

518
00:21:27.010 --> 00:21:28.910
One of them is Jim Halpert.

519
00:21:29.470 --> 00:21:35.430
But Jim notices someone come in to the office, and he has a big reaction, and he bails.

520
00:21:35.690 --> 00:21:38.750
He says, I'm not going to be your intro guy anymore, Andy.

521
00:21:38.750 --> 00:21:38.970
Yeah.

522
00:21:39.330 --> 00:21:41.230
